## What happened

Apple removed DSA support from its cryptographic frameworks (Security.framework / CommonCrypto). Starting in .NET 11, `DSA.Create()` throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS — the managed implementation no longer has a native backend to call on that platform. Windows and Linux still work because their OS-level crypto libraries (CNG / OpenSSL) still support DSA.

## How to fix it

You have two options, in order of preference:

### 1. Migrate to a modern algorithm (recommended)
Replace DSA with **ECDSA** or **Ed25519**. DSA is a legacy algorithm (FIPS 186-4 deprecated DSA for new signatures). If you control both signing and verification, re-sign with a modern key:

```csharp
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create(ECCurve.NamedCurves.nistP256);
byte[] sig = ecdsa.SignData(data, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

### 2. Use BouncyCastle for cross-platform DSA verification
If you **must** verify existing legacy DSA signatures (e.g., third-party data you can't re-sign), use **BouncyCastle**, which is a pure managed implementation with no OS dependency:

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

// Load your existing DSA public key parameters
var dsaParams = new DsaParameters(p, q, g);
var pubKey = new DsaPublicKeyParameters(y, dsaParams);

var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
verifier.Init(forSigning: false, pubKey);
verifier.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
bool valid = verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
```

This works identically on Linux, Windows, and macOS since it doesn't depend on OS crypto libraries.
